Which _does_ give you heretical thoughts about certain kinds
of infallibility.  Wouldn't at least gremlins, imps and the
ilk be likely to at least _peek_ into an unlocked unguarded
container on the off chance of stealing something?

[Granted that the turn counter sort of runs but doesn't do
much while the protagonist is off the level, at least the
concept that in certain parts of the dungeon you have to
_outrace_ the baddies to the goodies once you hit the level,
just like you must for stuff openly on the floor, seems
like it would add a bit of extra spice to the game, and add
value to potions of object detection used as originally
intended.

It does get complex, though, if done right.  Do you add
"knock" to the spells sophisticated NPC baddies or peacefuls
can have?  Key use? Lock too? Hmm, then why not Wizard Lock
for symmetry.  I'm not sure I'd like the coding chore, but I
think I'd like the results.  One of the ways the current
setup invites abuse is that just overloading a sack so most
probable monsters can't pick it up gives you the cheap cloth
equivalent of a chest, and that's a bit of a loophole,
though a stray nymph can ruin your whole game if you use it
much.]

